alright hey everyone how's it going I  hope you are well so today we're gonna  talk about rejection it's the getting  told no getting shut down maybe even  being ghosted so I'm not gonna give out  any relationship advice because I know  when I think of rejection the first  thing that I think about is dating but  yo girl hasn't dated in a long time so  we're gonna talk about all the other  methods of rejection things like career  related and life related goals and  dreams related kind of stuff I was at a  summit last week speaking on a panel and  someone in the audience asked what do  you do when you get told no and that  really made me think a little bit  because I think I started having kind of  newfound perspective on it and I just  wanted to talk about it and also just  because rejection happens a lot in this  career and other careers and in life and  stuff like for instance when you don't  get an offer from a company for a job or  an internship that you want an interview  for or getting ghosted by tons and tons  of recruiters when you're applying for  jobs and when you're on the job - it  happens a lot because maybe you're  getting told hey you can't build this  this way because that's just not how  things are done or maybe you didn't get  to work on a project that you were  passionate about because of other  reasons and I don't want to discount the  fact that rejection feels bad it sucks I  know it makes me feel sad and angry and  frustrated and like I'm not worthy and  the imposter syndrome kicks in it's just  not a fun time and I guess what I wanted  to do was just to provide some  perspective and advice for how to think  about rejection so that it feels a  little bit more positive and  constructive have hope everything's  gonna be okay so my answer to that  question at that panel was if you are  getting told no then just be curious and  find out why there are lots of reasons  for people to say no and before you pass  any judgment I think it's so important  to really figure out what is actually  going on here which honestly is just  kind of a theme in software engineering  when there's a problem you  just fix it you figure out what exactly  is the problem so that you make sure  that the solution that you have really  does fit that problem and I think it's  the same thing for getting rejected it  is really important to process your  feelings and if you get told knowing you  know take a step aside or sleep on it or  something just to get the feelings out  do it it's okay to cry it's okay to be  angry just let that out first because we  humans feel things and we need to let  them out in constructive and positive  ways and let ourselves feel things so  definitely do that I'm not saying that  we shouldn't do that because we can all  afford to let ourselves feel a little  more because we are human after all  so once you finish processing your  feelings which i think everybody should  do and you should definitely take the  time to do that because that is  important work figure out why you were  told no like for instance something that  I often do and I get rejected for jobs  is figure out okay what was it that I  didn't meet in their expectations or  even what even were their expectations  what kind of person were they looking  for  where are my gaps are those addressable  gaps that I can talk about and fix right  away or are they things that I should  set as career goals in the future so  that I minimize those gaps or like when  I get told that I can't work on a  project and I kind of dig into why I'll  realize it's because for instance  there's like 20 other projects that are  higher in priority that are more  important for the business and the  customer and for people inside the  company than the one that I was thinking  about and then it makes me think like oh  yeah like no that's fair that's logical  that makes sense I internalize it and  then that kind of rejection fear and  feelings just kind of go away but of  course kind of doing the work to figure  out the rejection can't happen without  communicating with other people there is  a lot of work that you can do by  yourself and just to analyze the  situation and really think about it from  all different points of view but at the  end of the day it's most likely that  there is a misunderstanding or  miscommunication on your part or theirs  and that's why it feels really bad to be  rejected in my series that I posted  earlier this year I talked a lot about  communication skills so I'll leave that  in a card up above but  for now I think that there are a lot of  good ways of approaching this situation  for how to ask why was I told no and the  first rule of thumb honestly is to not  ask why or why not  straight up I think depending on how you  ask it those questions tend to receive a  response that is more kind of on the  defensive side and it's probably not  gonna answer your question super well  when I think about when I'm asked like  hey why did you do this or why did you  make this decision it feels a little bit  like I have to defend myself and justify  my decision even though I fully well  known that I don't have all of the  context or the background I'll just try  my best to make the best decision at the  time since they're asking questions  around the who what when and where are  more constructive so instead of asking  why or why not then asking in ways that  are like hey I want to know a little bit  more about how this decision was made I  don't think I have a full picture here  so I would really appreciate that it's a  much more kinder way of asking that  question also things like hey what can I  do how can I improve who can I talk to  to get more information on this those  are also really great questions because  they turn into action questions they're  more like hey based on the answer what  can I do like what are things that I can  do to get that yes or to really  understand what's going on here so that  I'm helping you and you're helping me  and we're all just working together and  collaborating really well and I think  the other thing that really helps and I  super value this is vulnerability and I  know you all have heard me talk about  this before but being vulnerable and  honest and upfront about hey I've been  feeling this way and I just want to know  exactly why because I want this  opportunity or jawed because XYZ and I  would really appreciate it if you would  help me kind of lets that other person  into the situation and and empathize  with you a little bit and like if  someone's being vulnerable with you like  they're most likely not gonna shut you  down and I think another important thing  to remember and all this is that most  people are not rejecting you or  no because they're malicious they have  great intents they're just trying to do  their job really well based on the  information that they have in this white  ambiguous world that makes no sense and  the resources that they have and so I  think getting to understand that world  read from them and kind of getting to  know the world that they operate in is  just as valuable and those answers will  come out when you really dig into why  did this rejection come why was I told  no and I want to end on this Japanese  proverb which is non knuckle Dobby Yelp  ki which means fall down seven times and  get up eight I interpret this proverb as  both a sign of resilience of yeah you  might fall down seven times which is a  lot but so long as you get up eight  times then you're gonna be back on your  feet the other interpretation I have is  around learning every time that you fall  and you get up you learn something about  how you fell and how you're gonna get up  again and you do that over and over and  over and over again and I think it kind  of speaks to just like that's how life  is you fall and you get up and you fall  and get up like you've been doing this  since you were actually little like you  were actually falling and learning how  to get up when you were walking as well  as just all the ways that you failed but  maybe you didn't even realize that you  were failing you're just learning so  much along the way from failure and  failure is not a bad thing so long as  you're learning from those failures and  you're taking lessons from them and  you're introspecting and figuring out  what you can do better next time and I  know it's really cheesy but people say  that rejection is a gift failure is a  gift they celebrate failure and I think  the spirit of that is true many mistakes  is not a bad thing you're trying I think  trying and failing is way better than  not trying at all because every time  that you try and fail no matter how big  or small you're becoming a better  version of yourself or at least just the  next version of yourself and we're  evolving we are changing as human beings  no one stays the same person for years  and years and years like that's just  life and I think it's okay to celebrate  that and embrace it  and as really bad and sucky and shitty  as it is rejection is okay and it's a  good opportunity to reflect and feel  things and figure out what exactly is  happening here because you're probably  gonna learn a lot of great things along  the way okay so that's it for today I  hope you like this video also if you  have future video ideas let me know in  the comments as well I'm kind of  planning for what videos I want to make  in 2020 as we approach the end of 2019  which is so wild how fast this year flew  by and subscribe if you haven't done so  already click the notification bell and  that's it I think I hope you have a  great day wherever you are bye
 

